\title{An Infinite Cofinite Space Is Compact but Not Hausdorff}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    Let #{X} be infinite and carry the [cofinite topology](def-000E). Given an
    open cover of #{X}, choose one nonempty member #{U_0}. Its complement is
    finite. For each of the finitely many points outside #{U_0}, choose one
    further member of the cover containing that point. Together with #{U_0},
    these sets form a finite subcover, so #{X} is compact.
}
\p{
    Any two nonempty open subsets of #{X} intersect: the complement of their
    intersection is a union of two finite sets and cannot be all of the
    infinite set #{X}. Distinct points therefore cannot have disjoint open
    neighborhoods. Hence #{X} is not Hausdorff. The same observation shows
    that #{X} is connected.
}
